<<~ kanawai uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119`>>



! Oracle Governance

> Status: PROPOSED — design intent, mostly unbuilt. What stands of the read-face today:
> the Nexus's one public answer is the floor release, sealed by a seated Kahu key and
> served at the porch (`floor-release.ts`, `floor-answer.ts`, `floor-porch.ts`); the
> flow map keeps its own signed monotone pointer under the `flow-pointer` domain
> (`flow-substrate.ts`, `flow-read-client.ts`, `flow-read-face.ts`, route `/flow`). Each
> vessel's own document pointers rest in its own `@catalog` rows (`catalog-row.ts`), so
> no shared `@oracle` registry stands. The threshold-steward source and the three
> couplings below ride ahead of code; read each `@oracle` below as that public answer.

The `@oracle` carries a public read substrate — //what this neighborhood holds out for//
//all to see//. This meme answers ''who governs it, at what scale, and how it stays''
''un-capturable''. The short of it: ''the founder seeds, the circle governs, the fork''
''guards.''

''One public answer, two faces — never a second board.'' This governance board reads as the
''read face'' over the //same// substrate the registry/recipe plane already holds (the
genesis island document, still named `@oracle` until the floor read-face replaces its layer).
The ''content/registry face'' mints the recipe + engine-blob CIDs + protocol invariants
(<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-layer-ontology#/oracle-planes`>>); the
governed ''read face'' named here projects that content for public verify-only sight. The
two name one `@oracle` seen two ways — no reader should mistake this board for a second
`@oracle`.

<<~ ahu slot=`#/per-nexus-not-global`>>

!! Per-Nexus, Not One Global Board

An `@oracle` lives ''per-nexus'' — one public board per confederation — never one global
board for all of DreamNet. The grounds run ''OWNERSHIP and SCALE'', never no-global-now:

* ''Ownership'' — each nexus governs its own public surface; no single board accrues
  authority over a hundred thousand sovereign Lararia (that re-introduces the hub the
  watershed guardrail forbids,
  <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture#/rhizome-rhymes`>>).
* ''Scale'' — a planet-wide board concentrates write-pressure and read-fan into one
  bottleneck; per-nexus boards keep the lattice flat.

''Correct the no-global-now framing (MUST).'' A //global// `@oracle` does NOT violate
no-global-now. A shared `docId` names a ''rendezvous'' — an address, not a shared present.
Each peer that dials it ''materializes its own converging causal island'' over that
address; the address coordinates, the state stays local-first. //That IS no-global-now//
//enacted, not breached// (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/federated-causal-islands`>>).
So per-nexus rests on ownership and scale, never on a no-global-now prohibition the shared
address would not trigger anyway.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/board-model`>>

!! The Board Model — per-Nexus Address, Local Causal State

Each Nexus has one WHO board address, derived from its Nexus key. One porch answer per Nexus, the floor release, names the Nexus AID, the charter epoch, the release before it and the floor manifest CID; a seated Kahu key seals it as the steward, the cabal's approval of the manifest's exact bytes rides attached, the crossroads board carries it, and a herm carries and presents it without signing it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu`>> #/floor-release). No tender name reaches a stranger; the steward seal carries the authority.

The public floor exposes the system rows named by the genesis seed. Its read-face projects those rows alone as a history-free content-addressed snapshot; the snapshot carries the seed CID beside its own CID. The Kahu Plane road owes the floor's frozen seed-derived address and no-writer rule.

The Kahu Plane owns the steward-signing shape; R-WIRE owes the answer carriage. The board address derives without a pointer, so no public board document chooses its own address.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/steward-source-public-read`>>

!! Steward SOURCE → Public READ Projection

The board governs through a ''projection shore'', never a single writable doc:

* ''The SOURCE'' — a ''threshold-signed steward source'' the keeper-cabal-quorum holds.
  The cap-tier lives in ''WHO-CAN-SIGN-THE-SOURCE'', never in a write-cap on the published
  doc.
* ''The projection'' — the source projects to a ''verify-only public READ plane'' (the
  Two-Faced read-face, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture`>>;
  the TUF / DNSSEC shape: a signed source, a verifiable public mirror). Readers verify the
  projection against the steward signatures; they never write the published surface.

''NOT one cap-tiered doc (MUST-NOT).'' A single doc with a write-cap names a ''single''
''point of capture'' — steal the cap, own the board. The projection-nalu shore dissolves
that: a sovereign SOURCE → gated wave → public SINK, the read plane carrying no write
authority at all
(<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/projection-nalu`>>). ''Users read the''
''projection AND run their own topic boards'' off the well-known root — the board hands out
sight, never a write-throne.

moving the cap-tier from the //doc// to the //signing//
//quorum// keeps FLOW ⊥ WHO at the governance layer the same way the relay floor keeps it at
the carriage layer: the public can carry and read the projection without holding any
authority over it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/three-couplings`>>

!! Three Couplings — Founder Seeds, Circle Governs, Fork Guards

The board binds to its nexus through three orthogonal couplings, none collapsing into
another:

* ''① Origin.'' The root `docId` seeds from the ''founding daemon-plane'' — a content-bound
  birth, not a deed. ''Origin ≠ ownership'': the founder names the address; it does not
  thereby command the board forever. //The founder seeds.//
* ''② Governance.'' The ''keeper-cabal-quorum signs'' the steward source — a threshold
  subject composed as a closure over the keepers' independent signatures
  (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/group-as-closure`>>). No one keeper, and no
  one stolen key, moves the board. //The circle governs.//
* ''③ Liveness.'' The board rides ''content-addressed'' snapshots; the trust edge resolves
  by ''petname / TOFU introduction'' (home-nexus //listed//, never //required//). And the
  whole board stays ''forkable'' — the fork that guards: a capture, a charter the readers
  reject, a dead steward set, and the readers carry the content-addressed board onto a new
  steward source without permission. //The fork guards.//

the forkable backstop carries the deepest guarantee:
governance never needs to be perfect because exit stays cheap — content-addressing makes
the board portable, so capture costs the captor their readership, not the readers their
board.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/rendezvous-not-dht`>>

!! Rendezvous, Not a DHT — the DreamNet answer to provider discovery

The `@oracle` rendezvous carries the DreamNet answer to ''capability-gated provider''
''discovery WITHOUT a global DHT'' — the load-bearing question the content-resolution
design chased (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution`>>). A content
address names //what// a peer wants; //who holds it// resolves through the rendezvous, never
through a planet-wide index.

''Two independent research waves re-derived exactly this.'' From first principles, under
separate selection pressure, both landed on the same shape: ''a capability-gated''
''rendezvous — a relay-as-boundaried-meeting-point, never a global index.'' The
well-known-`docId` board that this meme governs IS that meeting-point: the address
coordinates the dial, and each peer materializes its own causal island of provider-state
behind it (the same global-address/local-state pattern the board model names above,
#board-model).

''The one structurally-forced global-now stays behind a causal-island boundary.'' A global
Kademlia DHT concentrates a shared present — every provider announcement racing one
routing table. That pressure does not enter the Lares stack proper: the `@oracle`
rendezvous ''replaces'' the DHT for discovery, and any DHT that survives sits //behind// a
causal-island boundary as an external bridge, never a load-bearing floor. The rendezvous
hands out an ''address''; the provider-state stays ''local-first''.

this reads as a ''convergence'', not a fresh invention:
the outside re-derived the inside. Two external waves, chasing capability-gated discovery
cold, arrived at the rendezvous the `@oracle` canon already stood — pattern-integrity
confirmed by independent arrival, the way a proof gains weight when a second hand reaches
it through a different door (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/docs/peer-discovery`>>).

<<~/ahu>>

